.TH PMC 2
.SH NAME
confpmc, rdpmc \- access to performance counters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.PP
.B
int confpmc(int core, int index, int mode, char *desc)
.PP
.B
uvlong rdpmc(int index)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Confpmc
configures the counter
.I index
of processor
.I core
(the one the process runs on, if
.I core
is negative):
it resets the counter, then sets what it counts to
.IR desc ,
one of the configurations
.IR pmc (3)
accepts (names of
.BR ctrdesc ,
or numbers depending on the architecture; nil leaves it),
and the
.IR mode ,
an OR of
.TP
.B PmcOs
count while in the kernel,
.TP
.B PmcUser
count while in user mode,
.TP
.B PmcEnable
start the counter.
.PP
.I Rdpmc
reads the counter
.I index
of the processor the process runs on.
.PP
Both use
.BR #ε ,
see
.IR pmc (3).
The process may move to another processor between two calls,
even within one (it finds its processor in
.BR #ε/core ,
then opens the counter), unless it is wired (see
.IR proc (3)).
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libc/9sys/confpmc.c
.br
.B /sys/src/libc/9sys/rdpmc.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pmc (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Confpmc
returns \-1 and sets the error string on failure;
.I rdpmc
returns
.B ~0
if it cannot read the counter.
.SH BUGS
.I Rdpmc
reads the file, a system call. NIX's
.I rdpmc
on amd64 used the
.B RDPMC
instruction, which faults where the kernel did not allow it;
programs may use the instruction themselves while a counter of their
processor is enabled.
